window.docContents[80].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___8-Identities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___8-Identities--___4___8-Identities--___4___8_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms":{"contents":"\\mathrm{Ln}(z_1z_2)=\\mathrm{Ln}z_1+\\mathrm{Ln}z_2.\nFor nonzero real arguments this is the real logarithm product law.\n\n\\ln(z_1z_2)=\\ln z_1+\\ln z_2,\\quad-\\pi\\leq\\operatorname{ph}z_1+\\operatorname{ph}z_2\\leq\\pi.\nPositive real arguments have zero phase, so the principal condition is automatic.\n\n\\mathrm{Ln}(z_1/z_2)=\\mathrm{Ln}z_1-\\mathrm{Ln}z_2.\nFor nonzero real arguments this is the real logarithm quotient law.\n\n\\ln(z_1/z_2)=\\ln z_1-\\ln z_2,\\quad-\\pi\\leq\\operatorname{ph}z_1-\\operatorname{ph}z_2\\leq\\pi.\nPositive real arguments have zero phase in this principal specialization.\n\n\\mathrm{Ln}(z^n)=n\\,\\mathrm{Ln}z,\\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z.\nThe integer exponent is represented by the real integer-power operation.\n\n\\ln(z^n)=n\\,\\ln z,\\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\quad-\\pi\\leq n\\operatorname{ph}z\\leq\\pi.\nFor a positive real base the phase condition is automatic.\n\n\\ln(1/z)=-\\ln z,\\quad|\\operatorname{ph}z|\\leq\\pi.\nFor positive real arguments the principal phase condition is automatic.\n\n\\mathrm{Ln}(\\exp z)=z+2k\\pi\\mathrm{i},\\quad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\nThe existential integer records the branch ambiguity of the complex logarithm.\n\n\\ln(\\exp z)=z,\\quad-\\pi\\leq\\Im z\\leq\\pi.\nMathlib's principal branch uses a strict lower boundary in the formal hypothesis.\n\n\\exp(\\ln z)=\\exp(\\mathrm{Ln}z)=z.\nFor every nonzero complex argument, exponentiating its principal logarithm recovers it.\n\n\\mathrm{Ln}(a^z)=z\\,\\mathrm{Ln}a+2k\\pi\\mathrm{i},\\quad a\\ne0.\nFor positive real base and real exponent there is no branch ambiguity.\n\n\\ln(a^z)=z\\,\\ln a+2k\\pi\\mathrm{i},\\quad a\\ne0.\nThe positive-real principal specialization removes the branch integer.\n\n\\ln(a^x)=x\\ln a,\\quad a>0.\nThis is the source's real-variable identity in its natural positive-base form.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.8 Identities\u0009§4.8 Identities","header":"§4.8(i) Logarithms","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___8-Identities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___8-Identities--___4___8-Identities--___4___8_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms"},"/Gamma-Function/___5___11-Asymptotic-Expansions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___11-Asymptotic-Expansions--___5___11_LPAR_iii_RPAR_-Ratios":{"contents":"In this subsection the source allows real or complex constants and approaches\nin a sector. The declarations below give positive-real specializations, so\nall powers and Gamma values use their unambiguous real meanings.\n\nThe generalized Bernoulli polynomials are selected by an explicit generating\nfunction specification. The ratio coefficients below are then defined from\nthat selected object, rather than chosen independently.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Gamma(z+a)}{\\Gamma(z+b)}\\sim z^{a-b}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Gamma(z+a)}{\\Gamma(z+b)}\\sim z^{a-b}\n\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{G_k(a,b)}{z^k}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Gamma(z+a)}{\\Gamma(z+b)}\\sim\n\\left(z+\\frac{a+b-1}{2}\\right)^{a-b}\n\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{H_k(a,b)}{(z+(a+b-1)/2)^{2k}}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\nG_0=1,\\quad G_1=\\frac12(a-b)(a+b-1),\\quad\nG_2=\\frac1{12}{a-b\\choose2}\\left(3(a+b-1)^2-(a-b+1)\\right).\n\\]The generalized binomial coefficient is written as a finite falling product.\n\n\\[\nH_0=1,\\quad H_1=-\\frac1{12}{a-b\\choose2}(a-b+1),\\quad\nH_2=\\frac1{240}{a-b\\choose4}\\left(2(a-b+1)+5(a-b+1)^2\\right).\n\\]\n\n\\[\nG_k(a,b)={a-b\\choose k}B_k^{(a-b+1)}(a).\n\\]\n\n\\[\nH_k(a,b)={a-b\\choose 2k}B_{2k}^{(a-b+1)}\\left(\\frac{a-b+1}{2}\\right).\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Gamma(z+a)\\Gamma(z+b)}{\\Gamma(z+c)}\\sim\n\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^k\\frac{(c-a)_k(c-b)_k}{k!}\n\\Gamma(a+b-c+z-k).\n\\]On positive real z, the summand is exposed as gammaProductTerm, using the\nsame finite rising factorial as 5.11.5.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function\u0009§5.11 Asymptotic Expansions","header":"§5.11(iii) Ratios","id":"/Gamma-Function/___5___11-Asymptotic-Expansions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___11-Asymptotic-Expansions--___5___11_LPAR_iii_RPAR_-Ratios"}});